          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"}> commutes
            \. Since there is an obvious notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            for such
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
            %em category of discrete time stochastic processes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi dStoch
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{dStoch}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Stoch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Stoch}
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"}> Stoch
            ) to mean the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Meas
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Meas}_G
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}(G)
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+process"} Markov stochastic processes
          %p
            In the preceding diagram the morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+morphisms"} Kleisli morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi k
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Q
                        %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\prod_{k \lt n} \Omega_k \xrightarrow{Q_n} \Omega_n
            are called
            %strong dynamic laws
            (of motion), and stochastic processes are classified by properties of the dynamic laws.
          %p
            A
            %strong Markov dynamic law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_n
            is a dynamic law depending only on the current state, i.e.,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_n
