                  %use{:x => "171.601503", "xlink:href" => "#KTukpbY0NgvCrdnymU2HVhXhSm4=-glyph1-3", :y => "47.042047"}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{n-1}
            is the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"}> measurable
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            which specifies the deterministic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+morphism"} Kleisli morphism
            (with the same notation). Thus a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+process"} Markov dynamic law
            only depends upon the current state and not its history.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Q}
            is a stochastic process such that the dynamic law at every stage is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+process"} Markov dynamic law
            we say the stochastic process
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Q}
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+process"} Markov stochastic process
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Omega}
            defines a constant sequence,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_n = \Omega
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Omega}
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+chain"} Markov chain
          %p
            An even more elementary way of defining a stochastic process is by taking
            = succeed "," do
